      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find the perimeter of the larger triangle, we first need to determine the scale factor between the two similar triangles. Since the longest side of the original triangle is 10 cm, and the longest side of the larger triangle is 25 cm, we can calculate the scale factor as follows:\n\nScale factor = (Longest side of larger triangle) / (Longest side of original triangle)\nScale factor = 25 cm / 10 cm\nScale factor = 2.5\n\nNow that we have the scale factor, we can find the lengths of the other two sides of the larger triangle. Since the original triangle is isosceles, both of the equal sides are 8 cm long. Using the scale factor, we can calculate the lengths of the other two sides of the larger triangle as follows:\n\nNew side length = Original side length × Scale factor\nNew side length = 8 cm × 2.5\nNew side length = 20 cm\n\nNow that we have the lengths of all three sides of the larger triangle, we can find its perimeter:\n\nPerimeter of larger triangle = Sum of all three side lengths\nPerimeter of larger triangle = 20 cm + 20 cm + 25 cm\nPerimeter of larger triangle = 65 cm\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the larger triangle is 65 cm.\nThe answer is: 65",
